# LibreChat Data Dashboard

This project exposes an Apache ECharts dashboard backed by the LibreChat MongoDB database. The dashboard surfaces headline metrics and operational activity breakdowns by querying the database through an Express API.

## Features

- Total counts for agents, users, and messages.
- Agent distribution by category with automatic label resolution.
- Top message authors rendered with the users' first and last names instead of raw identifiers.
- Daily time series for message traffic, active users, new users, and login activity (based on `updatedAt`).
- Operational views for messages per agent, conversations per agent, and message distribution by endpoint to highlight adoption patterns.
- Interactive date slicer (single day or range) that refines every chart on demand and reports filter status inline.
- One-click Excel export covering the headline metrics and all chart datasets for offline analysis.
- Responsive layout with animated cards and enlarged chart canvases powered by Apache ECharts.

## Getting started

1. Install dependencies:

   ```bash
   npm install
   ```

2. Create a `.env` file based on `.env.example` and update it with your MongoDB connection details.
   - You can either paste a full `MONGODB_URI`/`MONGO_URI` (make sure any special characters in the password are percent encoded) or provide the host/port/username/password values separately—the server will safely encode them for you.
   - When connecting to the hosted LibreChat deployment, set:

     ```env
     MONGODB_HOST=hcux404.teagasc.net
     MONGODB_PORT=27018
     MONGODB_USERNAME=admin
     MONGODB_PASSWORD=TeagascICT2025@!
     MONGODB_AUTH_SOURCE=admin
     ```

   - If you're running LibreChat locally with the default `docker-compose` configuration (which starts MongoDB with `--noauth`), leave the username and password blank. The dashboard automatically retries without credentials when authentication is disabled.

3. Start the server:

   ```bash
   npm start
   ```

4. Visit [http://localhost:3000](http://localhost:3000) to view the dashboard.

## Development

- `npm run dev` launches the Express server with hot reload via `nodemon`.
- The public folder hosts the static dashboard assets (`index.html`, `app.js`, and `styles.css`).
- API responses are served from `/api/dashboard` and `/api/health`.

## Notes

- If authentication fails, double-check that the credentials in your `.env` file match the database user and that any special characters are encoded (the split variable approach handles this automatically). When the server detects an authentication failure and you have provided credentials, it will retry without them in case the target MongoDB instance is running without auth.
- The dashboard expects the LibreChat database to expose the collections: `users`, `agents`, `agentcategories`, `messages`.
- The `usersLoggedPerDay` metric is derived from the `updatedAt` field as a proxy for login activity.
- Date filters operate on the `createdAt` field for message-derived analytics and the `updatedAt` field for user activity; clearing the filters restores the full-range view.
- Excel exports include the currently filtered datasets. Apply the desired date range before exporting to capture that slice of activity.
